e": "text", "content": "As you read this sentence, your intonation should partially fall at the end of the each thought group, except for the final one, when your voice falls to indicate that you are finished. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Practice reading these sentences from the text:"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Regardless, he called his girlfriend to apologize, and to beg her forgiveness, but she refused to speak to him."}, {"type": "example", "content": "We talked about how you are always so inconsiderate, how she always puts up with your guff, and how you always get on her nerves. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "I asked her whether she was sick of your hostility, if she wanted to try to forget about you and whether she was certain that she wouldn't have any regrets."}, {"type": "example", "content": "I asked her whether she would miss you, and if she still loved you despite all of the things you had done. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "I asked her whether she wanted you to call her, if she had a message for you, and why she was so enraged."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": " Changing Emphasis"}, {"type": "describer", "content": " The way you order clauses and words within sentences can change the way the idea is presented. You can use these strategies to emphasize certain elements that you're trying to communicate. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "  <span class=\"underline\">Passive constructions</span>  vary the way information is given in a sentence, putting more emphasis on what comes first.  <br/><br/> <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">All roads to the north</span>  <span class=\"underline\">have been blocked </span>by police. "}, {"type": "text", "content": " <span class=\"underline\">Inversion</span> refers to changing the normal word order in the sentence so that a prepositional phrase is emphasised before the verb. This also involves putting the verb before the subject.  <br/><br/><span class=\"italic\"> Suddenly <span class=\"strong\">down came</span> the rain! <br/> <span class=\"strong\">Up in the air</span> went the balloon. </span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": " <span class=\"underline\">Fronting</span> involves changing the order of clauses in a sentence and putting a clause first that would usually not be first. <br/><br/> <span class=\"italic\">I don't know where the money is coming from. <br/> <span class=\"strong\">Where the money is coming from</span>, I don't know. </span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": " <span class=\"underline\">Time phrases</span>  can vary in position, and are often put first because the time reference is important.  <br/><br/><span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\"> At six o'clock</span> Maria decided to phone the office."}, {"type": "text", "content": "  <span class=\"underline\">What clauses </span><br/><br/>  These are common with verbs such as  <span class=\"italic\">need</span>,  <span class=\"italic\">want</span>,  <span class=\"italic\">like</span>,  <span class=\"italic\">hate</span>. <br/><br/>  <span class=\"italic\">I hate the snow. <br/> <span class=\"strong\">What I hate is</span> the snow.<br/> You need a vacation. <br/> <span class=\"strong\">What you need is</span> a vacation.</span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "What exactly the cause of the problem was, he did not know."}, {"type": "example", "content": "It seemed as though all possible options had been exhausted."}, {"type": "example", "content": "What she hates most is your constant nagging."}, {"type": "example", "content": "As to where all of this animosity is coming from, she could not say."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "forgiveness"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "stopping one's feelings of anger or resentmen"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Regardless, he called Elizabeth to apologize, and to beg her forgiveness."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le08ob04aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": " These are words you use to talk about relationship problems."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "guff"}, {"type": "definition", "content": " trivial, worthless, or insolent talk or ideas"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We talked about how you are always so inconsiderate, how she always puts up with your guff, and how you always get on her nerves."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le08ob04aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to get on one's nerves "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to irritate or annoy someone"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We talked about how you are always so inconsiderate, how she always puts up with your guff, and how you always get on her nerves."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le08ob04aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to nag"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to persistently find fault or continuously urge "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: What she hates most is your constant nagging. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le08ob04aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "animosity"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "strong hostility"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: As to where all of this animosity is coming from, she could not say."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le08ob04aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "enraged"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "very angry; furious"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content